  "narrative": {
    "essence": "Scotland hasn't been to a World Cup since 1998 — nearly three decades of heartbreak, near-misses, and pub-bench philosophy about what might have been. They're back. And they're in the group of death. The Tartan Army doesn't care. This is what they live for.",
    "footballIdentity": "Scottish football is suffering and love in equal measure. The country invented the passing game. They drew 0-0 with England at Wembley in 1967 and called themselves world champions. They've been eliminated from every World Cup at the group stage, usually in spectacular and emotionally devastating fashion. But Scotland's fans — the Tartan Army — are famous worldwide for their good-natured carnival spirit. They party because the football might make them cry.",
    "theMoment": "Scotland qualified ending a 28-year World Cup absence and are drawn into one of the most difficult groups imaginable — Brazil and Morocco in the same group. But Steve Clarke has transformed this team: from perennial EURO qualifier squandering to a disciplined, organized unit that believes in itself. The independence referendum debate that consumed Scottish politics for a decade has settled into something quieter, but the national football team has become a unifying force regardless of constitutional preference.",
    "momentType": "historical"
  },
