  "tactics": {
    "styleTags": [
      "Organized",
      "Physical",
      "Set pieces",
      "Direct play"
    ],
    "tacticalNote": "Clarke's 3-5-2 gives Scotland defensive solidity with Robertson bombing down the left as an outlet. The wing-backs are critical — without them pushing high, Scotland lack width. McTominay and McGinn provide central energy while the front two work as a pressing pair. Scotland can make life very difficult for better teams through organization and intensity.",
    "strength": "Defensive discipline and set piece danger — Scotland is organized, hard to score against, and generates threatening moments from dead balls.",
    "vulnerability": "Technical quality against elite pressing teams — when Scotland can't establish the wing-backs, they get pinned back and have limited ways out."
  },
  "watchFor": [
    "Robertson's output down the left flank — he essentially plays as a left winger and Scotland's attack runs through his overlapping runs.",
    "McTominay's late arrivals into the penalty area — he's scored pivotal goals appearing from deep at the right moment.",
    "How Scotland handles Brazil's press — this is the tactical puzzle of the group. Scotland must work the ball quickly to avoid being trapped."
  ],
